WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.610 --> 00:00:07.420
MARKUS KLUTE: All right,
so welcome back to 8.801.

00:00:07.420 --> 00:00:10.720
We'll continue our discussion
on Feynman calculus.

00:00:10.720 --> 00:00:14.200
And here we dive
into toy theory.

00:00:14.200 --> 00:00:16.390
So this theory is a toy.

00:00:16.390 --> 00:00:20.710
And it's just an example to
illustrate Feynman rules.

00:00:20.710 --> 00:00:23.370
What we do, the
simplification we employ here

00:00:23.370 --> 00:00:26.260
is leaving out the spin
of the particle involved.

00:00:26.260 --> 00:00:31.430
We consider the spin, we add
another algebraic complication

00:00:31.430 --> 00:00:33.072
which is quite now confusing.

00:00:33.072 --> 00:00:34.280
So we leave this out for now.

00:00:34.280 --> 00:00:36.250
We come back later to this.

00:00:36.250 --> 00:00:38.950
So we're supposed to have three
kinds of particles involved

00:00:38.950 --> 00:00:41.770
here-- particle A,
B, and C. And so we

00:00:41.770 --> 00:00:45.280
can have a primary vertex where
these all three particle are

00:00:45.280 --> 00:00:47.840
interacting like shown here.

00:00:47.840 --> 00:00:54.970
So particle A, might decay
into particle B and C.

00:00:54.970 --> 00:00:56.950
You can assume that
particle A is heavier

00:00:56.950 --> 00:01:01.510
than the sum of B and C, so
this is schematically allowed.

00:01:01.510 --> 00:01:05.840
We might also have corrections
involved here as shown here.

00:01:05.840 --> 00:01:08.230
And what we'd be interested
in is now, for example,

00:01:08.230 --> 00:01:12.480
calculating the lifetime
of this particle A.

00:01:12.480 --> 00:01:15.990
We might do this just for
this primitive vertex.

00:01:15.990 --> 00:01:20.370
Or we might do this for this
complicated set of corrections.

00:01:20.370 --> 00:01:24.030
We might also be interested
in calculating scattering

00:01:24.030 --> 00:01:26.670
processes where particle A
is scattered with particle A

00:01:26.670 --> 00:01:29.460
and produces particle
B and particle B.

00:01:29.460 --> 00:01:33.220
Or we scatter particle A
with particle B and so on.

00:01:33.220 --> 00:01:36.250
So in this theory, at
the end of the lecture,

00:01:36.250 --> 00:01:39.450
we have all tools in
hand to calculate this.

00:01:39.450 --> 00:01:42.660
No worries-- I will not
leave you alone with this.

00:01:42.660 --> 00:01:45.240
This lecture, we go
through the recipe.

00:01:45.240 --> 00:01:48.400
And then later on we'll see
how we actually apply this.

00:01:48.400 --> 00:01:50.680
So let's look at this recipe.

00:01:50.680 --> 00:01:52.800
So the recipe has
a number of steps.

00:01:52.800 --> 00:01:57.210
And the key is to just
follow those steps in order

00:01:57.210 --> 00:01:59.940
to get to the desired result.

00:01:59.940 --> 00:02:02.670
The first step is to label
incoming and outgoing

00:02:02.670 --> 00:02:04.560
four-momenta of particles.

00:02:04.560 --> 00:02:09.600
We label them with
p1, p2, and up to pn.

00:02:09.600 --> 00:02:12.520
We also want to label
all internal momenta.

00:02:12.520 --> 00:02:14.850
So we have an
internal line, then we

00:02:14.850 --> 00:02:19.920
want to label this internal
momenta with q1, q2, and so on.

00:02:19.920 --> 00:02:21.840
We want to add
arrows to each line

00:02:21.840 --> 00:02:25.650
to keep track of what is a
positive direction, as we

00:02:25.650 --> 00:02:29.040
discussed before, particles
might travel backwards in time.

00:02:29.040 --> 00:02:31.080
Those are typically
antiparticles.

00:02:31.080 --> 00:02:34.530
And for those we, have to
make sure that we correctly

00:02:34.530 --> 00:02:37.170
account for the momenta.

00:02:37.170 --> 00:02:42.170
For each vertex,
we have a factor.

00:02:42.170 --> 00:02:45.170
We write this factor
minus ig, where

00:02:45.170 --> 00:02:47.100
g is the coupling constant.

00:02:47.100 --> 00:02:48.740
So this is a measure
of the strength

00:02:48.740 --> 00:02:51.540
of the interaction involved.

00:02:51.540 --> 00:02:52.790
Then we have a propagator.

00:02:52.790 --> 00:02:55.580
So for each internal
line, the internal lines

00:02:55.580 --> 00:02:56.990
are also called propagator.

00:02:56.990 --> 00:03:02.360
We write down a factor, i
over qj squared minus mj

00:03:02.360 --> 00:03:03.890
squared t squared.

00:03:03.890 --> 00:03:08.710
Note that qj
squared doesn't have

00:03:08.710 --> 00:03:12.280
to be mj squared c squared,
meaning that the parity can

00:03:12.280 --> 00:03:15.980
be off shell, off mass shell.

00:03:15.980 --> 00:03:18.573
You also see that there is a
complication in the integral

00:03:18.573 --> 00:03:20.740
when you actually have those
vectors being the same.

00:03:24.740 --> 00:03:27.320
You want to make sure that
energy and momentum is

00:03:27.320 --> 00:03:28.080
conserved.

00:03:28.080 --> 00:03:34.790
So for each vertex, you
write down a delta function

00:03:34.790 --> 00:03:35.670
with the conditions.

00:03:35.670 --> 00:03:39.770
This is for this three vertex
where momentum of the first one

00:03:39.770 --> 00:03:42.950
plus the second plus
the third is equal to 0.

00:03:42.950 --> 00:03:46.580
Only then the value of
the delta function is 1.

00:03:46.580 --> 00:03:48.710
Remember, there's a
minus sign somewhere,

00:03:48.710 --> 00:03:53.630
most likely here
for this k1 value.

00:03:53.630 --> 00:03:56.550
Then you want to integrate
overall internal momenta.

00:03:56.550 --> 00:04:00.530
So for each internal
line, we write a factor--

00:04:00.530 --> 00:04:04.070
1 over 2 pi to the fourth power.

00:04:04.070 --> 00:04:06.980
d4 is on your momenta.

00:04:06.980 --> 00:04:11.690
And then, this all will
result in a delta function,

00:04:11.690 --> 00:04:13.670
which you just eliminate.

00:04:13.670 --> 00:04:17.089
And you do that
by multiplying it.

00:04:17.089 --> 00:04:20.810
You erase this delta
function and you replace it

00:04:20.810 --> 00:04:23.090
by a factor i.

00:04:23.090 --> 00:04:24.830
So this seems like
very confusing.

00:04:24.830 --> 00:04:27.110
Why do you add delta
functions first, and then

00:04:27.110 --> 00:04:28.460
you erase them later?

00:04:28.460 --> 00:04:38.350
Note in Fermi's
golden rule, we use

00:04:38.350 --> 00:04:40.430
the squared of the amplitude.

00:04:40.430 --> 00:04:42.790
And you also saw that the
[? phase-based ?] factors

00:04:42.790 --> 00:04:48.160
already have this kind of
delta functions included.

00:04:48.160 --> 00:04:50.710
So we get out of the
complications that we

00:04:50.710 --> 00:04:53.650
don't really know what to
square of the delta function is

00:04:53.650 --> 00:04:58.570
by erasing, by adding the
i and then keeping track

00:04:58.570 --> 00:05:03.580
of the momentum conservation,
this conservation here when we

00:05:03.580 --> 00:05:05.230
apply the
[? phase-based ?] factor.

00:05:05.230 --> 00:05:09.940
And then, voila, you just
calculated a matrix element.

00:05:09.940 --> 00:05:12.370
All right, so those
are the rules.

00:05:12.370 --> 00:05:16.740
Now the key is to practice
how to apply them.

00:05:16.740 --> 00:05:19.670
So what we do next is to
practice using this toy

00:05:19.670 --> 00:05:23.060
experiment in how to
calculate the matrix element,

00:05:23.060 --> 00:05:26.970
the [? phase ?] base, and the z
decay rates and cross sections.

00:05:26.970 --> 00:05:30.750
And then as a following step, we
will see how this all unfolds.

00:05:30.750 --> 00:05:32.510
Then we have a real
series, like QED,

00:05:32.510 --> 00:05:36.910
like the weak interaction
and the strong interaction.

